{"id": "74b195f5-3417-4bb5-8210-7e828128702c", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "**The deny comes from the permissions boundary on the role.** It isn't a gap in `AmazonBedrockFullAccess`.

The Lambda's execution role is `llmops-lf-app-boundary-role`. It has `AmazonBedrockFullAccess` and `AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole` attached, with no inline policies. It also has a permissions boundary, `llmops-lf-boundary` (default version v4). That policy reads:

```json
{
  "Statement": [
    { "Effect": "Allow", "Action": "*", "Resource": "*" },
    { "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": ["bedrock:InvokeModel*", "bedrock:Converse*"],
      "Resource": "*" }
  ]
}
```

The explicit Deny covers `bedrock:InvokeModel`, `InvokeModelWithResponseStream` and the `Converse*` actions on every resource. An explicit deny in a boundary overrides any Allow from the identity policies, so `AmazonBedrockFullAccess` can't grant these actions on this role.

The boundary policy was last modified today at 14:50 UTC. The role also has the tag `llmops-eval-fault = bc-iam-boundary`, so this may be a deliberately injected fault for an evaluation.

**To fix it,** remove the `Deny` statement from `llmops-lf-boundary`, or narrow it to the models or actions you actually want blocked. You can also detach the boundary from this role. I can't change IAM policies or permissions, so someone with IAM admin rights needs to make that change. The boundary applies to one role (`PermissionsBoundaryUsageCount` is 1), so editing it won't affect other roles.

I didn't check SCPs or Bedrock model access. Since the boundary explains the denial, I stopped there. If `InvokeModel` still fails after the boundary is fixed, SCPs would be the next layer to check. The error message's "explicit deny in a permissions boundary" wording would also confirm this diagnosis.", "type": "text"}], "created_at": null}

